{"kind":"request","text":"Kernel K9: tie the definition validator to the definition format (docs/kernel.md, section 5).\n\nWhy. Replay checks each pinned definition with today's validator (packages/replay/src/verify.ts:1010). A stricter check in packages/derive/src/validate makes an old history `unsupported-definition`. Kernel promises 2 and 3 (docs/kernel.md, section 6) fail in that case. R7 (0716e75a) adds the first planned stricter checks, so this item comes before it.\n\nWork:\n1. One rule, stated in the head: each definition format (today `artroom-definition-1`) names one set of validator checks. A check that refuses a definition that its format accepts today goes only into a new format, or into a new version of the optional part that owns it (level, capacity, profile or a capability). Put the rule in docs/kernel.md K9 and in the scope contract section that defines the format. Say how the code holds it.\n2. A corpus of pinned definitions: every declared definition in apps/, examples/, fixtures and recorded histories under test. One test validates each one under its format and names each definition that is refused. Say how a new pinned definition joins the corpus.\n3. Replay selects the validator by the pinned definition's format. An unknown format gives `unsupported-definition`, and the reason names the format.\n\nWitnesses: the corpus passes at the head. One paired control: a temporary stricter format-1 check makes the corpus test fail and name the definition; the control is not committed. Replay of a definition with an unknown format gives `unsupported-definition` with the format named.\n\nKernel placement: this head closes K9. It adds the format-to-validator binding to the kernel and nothing else: no scope kind, platform name, capability, tool family or record model. Its review cites docs/kernel.md section 7.","body":{"conditions":"Closes K9 only. No change to any pinned definition, digest or recorded entry. No deployment. One gate at the head; checker revie